TCP Flags:

SYN | Synchronize               | Initiate a connection
ACK | Acknowledge               | Confirm receipt of data
FIN | Finish                    | Gracefully close a connection
RST | Reset                     | Abruptly terminate a connection
PSH | Push                      | Deliver data immediately(don't buffer)
URG | Urgent                    | Prioritize marked data
ECE | ECN Echo                  | Signal congestion(used with CWR)
CWR | Congestion Window Reduced | Confirm congestion response

Connection Lifecycle:

3-Way Handshake(open):            4-Way Teardown(close):
                                  
Client      Server                Client      Server
|---SYN------->| seq=x            |---FIN------->| client done sending
|<--SYN+ACK----| seq=y, ack=x+1   |<--ACK--------|
|---ACK------->| ack=y+1          |<--FIN--------| server done sending
| ESTABLISHED  |                  |---ACK------->|
                                  |    CLOSED    | client waits TIME_WAIT(2xMSL) before fully 
                                                   closing

RST - abrupt reset (no handshake): sent on invalid packets, refused ports, or crashed peers.

Connection States:

CLOSED -> LISTEN -> SYN_RCVD -------------------|
                                                |
CLOSED -> SYN_SENT ----------------------> ESTABLISHED
                                                |
                  |-----------------------------|
              FIN_WAIT_1                    CLOSE_WAIT
                  |                             |
              FIN_WAIT_2                     LAST_ACK
                  |                             |
              TIME_WAIT<----------------------CLOSED
                  |
                CLOSED

Congestion Control - 4 Phases

1. Slow Start:
  a) Begin with cwnd = 1 MSS;
  b) Double cwnd every RTT(Exponential growth);
  c) Until cwnd hits ssthresh or loss detected;
2. Congestion Avoidance:
  a) Once cwnd > ssthresh;
  b) Grow linearly: cwnd += 1 MSS per RTT;
  c) "Additive Increase";
3. Fast Retransmit:
  a) On 3 duplicate ACKs -> retransmit immediately(don't wait for timeout);
  b) Assumes single packets loss, not full congestion;
4. Fast Recovery(Reno/CUBIC):
  a) After 3 dup ACKs:
    ssthresh = cwnd / 2;
    cwnd - ssthresh + 3;
    Resume congestion avoidance(don't drop to slow start);
  b) After timeout(severe): cwnd = 1, restart slow start;

CUBIC(Linux default)
Instead of purely ACK-driven, CUBIC grows cwnd as a cubic function of time since last loss:
  W(t) = C * (t - K)^3 + Wmax
Wmax = Window before last reduction;
K = time to reach Wmax again;
Aggressive on fast/long links, backs off near Wmax - better for high-bandwidth networks
than Reno;

cwnd(Congestion Window) is a TCP mechanism that limits the amout of data a sender can
transmit before receiving acknowledgements(ACKs), preventing network congestion.

ssthresh(Slow Start Theshold) is a TCP congestion control variable that defines
the limit between exponential growth(slow start) and linear growth(congestion avoidance)
of the congestion window(cwnd). When cwnd reaches ssthresh, TCP becomes more conservative,
increasing the window by 1 MSS per RTT to avoid network congestion.

MSS(Maximum Segment Size).

rwnd(Receive Window) a key parameter in TCP flow control that prevents a sender from
overwhelming a receiver with too much data.

                             Flow Control               Congestion Control
##################################################################################
Goal                | Don't overwhelm the reciever | Don't overwhelm the network
Mechanism           | Reciever advertises rwnd     | Sender manages cwnd
Signal              | TCP window field in header   | Packet loss / ECN
Effective send rate | main(cwnd, rwnd)             | -
